// builtins.memql
//
// Procedure learning's entry points (design record
// docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-13-app-session-recording-and-learning-program-design.md,
// section 4 epics C and D). Executors live in integrations/procedure; the pure
// algorithms they call live in component/procedure and component/work, and
// reach no model.
//
// NONE CARRIES @serverOnly, AND THAT IS NOT AN OVERSIGHT. @serverOnly is
// refused on a builtin at parse -- it is accepted on a query and a mutation --
// so the gate each of these needs lives in its HANDLER, which is the same place
// the log store's reads put theirs and for the same reason: a builtin's
// annotation set carries no rank or origin gate.
//
// The gates they need are real. Learning writes a construct into somebody's
// catalog with a goalSignature attached, which is the key compile's
// exact-match tier serves a later goal from WITHOUT A MODEL; the ladder
// builtins move a procedure toward serving goals unreviewed. A caller able to
// drive either on somebody else's rows could point a goal at a template of
// their choosing. The handlers therefore run every read under the NAMED owner's
// actor, admit the maintenance principal (component/auth/maintenance_actor.go)
// only where a scheduled or event-fired automation has no person to name, and
// reach the @serverOnly authoring mutations only through the one
// internal-origin stamp in store.go -- so a caller who is not that owner reads
// zero rows and moves nothing.

/// Mine the corpus a finished run belongs to. Reads that run's owner's recorded runs for the same
/// goal signature, mines the recurring shape, generalizes it into a template with classified holes,
/// and -- when an abstraction clears the compression floor of at least two uses -- lifts it into a
/// validated bundle carrying its provenance. Nothing auto-activates: the construct enters the
/// certification ladder on the rung its evidence earns, and only a person's approval moves it from
/// shadow toward serving. Returns {runId, goalSignature, sequences, candidates, constructId,
/// accepted, reason}. A corpus of fewer than two runs is the ORDINARY answer, not a failure.
@executor("integration.procedure.learnFromRun")
builtin procedureLearnFromRun {
  runId  string!  @description("The v1:work:run that just succeeded.")
  level  integer  @description("1 mines the actions inside recorded sessions (default); 2 mines automation invocations, so a repeated sequence of automations becomes a higher automation.")
}

/// The scheduled sweep: mine one owner's recorded corpus for one goal signature at one level.
/// Disliked recordings are excluded entirely. Returns {ownerUserId, goalSignature, level,
/// sequences, candidates, constructId, accepted, reason}.
@executor("integration.procedure.mineCorpus")
builtin procedureMineCorpus {
  ownerUserId    string!  @description("Whose corpus to mine. Every read runs as this person: the composite owner tier would otherwise blend two people's recordings into one template.")
  goalSignature  string   @description("Restrict to one goal's runs. Empty mines every signature the owner has.")
  level          integer  @description("1 actions (default); 2 automation invocations.")
}

/// The statement every step of a rendered learned procedure is (epic memql#5408). The lift renders a
/// procedure as MemQL source -- the auditable artifact a promotion approval pins beside the template
/// and the preconditions -- and each of its steps is one call to this builtin, naming the recorded
/// action and its arguments with every hole spelled as what it stands for.
///
/// IT NEVER RUNS AS AN ORDINARY STATEMENT: the handler refuses outside a replay ("procedureStep runs
/// only inside replayLearnedProcedure"). A learned procedure is served through the certification
/// ladder and never activated as an automation, because the ladder's shadow evidence and its one
/// human approval are what stand between a recording and an action taken again on somebody's
/// machine. What a replay executes is the template stored on the construct; this statement is how
/// the source SAYS what that template does.
@executor("integration.procedure.step")
builtin procedureStep {
  step  integer!  @description("The step's index into the procedure template's steps, as the lift rendered it.")
  tool  string!   @description("The action the app took: exec, fs_write, fs_read, fetch or mcp.")
  args  object    @description("The action's arguments, every hole spelled as the goal input, constant or earlier result it stands for.")
}

/// Serve the current work run from a learned procedure (epic memql#5408) -- the one statement of
/// replayLearnedProcedure. The handler reads the run it executes in and refuses outside one,
/// refuses a construct owned by anybody but that run's owner, and RE-READS the construct's rung: a
/// procedure demoted or re-lifted since compile chose it falls back to the app instead of replaying.
/// Otherwise it binds the goal's input to the procedure's free parameters, checks the learned
/// preconditions on the target, and replays the template step by step -- stopping at the first
/// divergence and handing the app the steps that already ran, so none is repeated. Returns
/// {servedBy, constructId, rung, diverged, repairRunId}; servedBy is procedure or app.
@executor("integration.procedure.replay")
builtin procedureReplay {
  constructId  string!  @description("The learned procedure to replay: a v1:authoring:construct owned by the run's owner.")
}

/// One of the certification ladder's two maintenance sweeps (epic memql#5408). `demotion`
/// re-evaluates every canary and trusted procedure's stored evidence against the CURRENT ladder
/// policy, so a threshold that tightened demotes a procedure without waiting for its next replay;
/// `retirement` retires a procedure unused for longer than the policy's window, on any rung. The
/// handler refuses every caller but the cluster's maintenance principal and trusted server-side Go,
/// and reads each owner's procedures under that owner's own actor.
@executor("integration.procedure.ladderSweep")
builtin procedureLadderSweep {
  sweep  enum("demotion", "retirement")!  @description("Which sweep to run.")
}

/// Apply a person's decision on a procedurePromotion approval to the ladder (epic memql#5408):
/// approved moves the procedure from shadow to canary, rejected keeps it in shadow and makes it
/// re-earn the proposal. IDEMPOTENT -- a construct no longer waiting on this approval, or no longer in
/// shadow, is left exactly as it is -- and it never touches the finished shadow run the approval
/// names.
@executor("integration.procedure.decidePromotion")
builtin procedureDecidePromotion {
  approvalId  string!  @description("The decided v1:work:approval, of kind procedurePromotion.")
}
